// paideia-as#1354 — crypto trait declarations moved to per-primitive
// sibling files.
//
// The v0.30-and-earlier landings kept all three crypto primitives'
// trait declarations in this one file. Wave-0 parallelism for the
// v0.25-v0.32 crypto substrate (see MASTER_PLAN.md §13.4) requires
// that adding a new primitive not touch a shared file, so the trait
// declarations now live one-per-file under `crypto/`:
//
//   - crypto/argon2id.pdx           — RFC 9106 Argon2id KDF.
//   - crypto/chacha20_poly1305.pdx  — RFC 8439 ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD.
//   - crypto/ml_kem_768.pdx         — FIPS 203 ML-KEM-768 KEM.
//
// The elaborator's `stdlib_lowering::cryptoops` dispatcher (also
// split per-primitive at paideia-as#1354) routes calls from `.pdx`
// callers to the extern-C thunks under
// `crates/paideia-as-crypto/src/ffi/`. Every trait / thunk pair is
// spelt the same way on both sides of the FFI boundary so a reader
// tracing a call site from `.pdx` to Rust never has to memorise a
// translation table.
//
// # Adding a new crypto primitive (append-only recipe)
//
// 1. Add a per-primitive Rust FFI file at
//    `crates/paideia-as-crypto/src/ffi/<name>.rs` (thunk + repr-C
//    parameter bundle + tests).
// 2. Declare the module + re-export the thunks at
//    `crates/paideia-as-crypto/src/ffi/mod.rs` (append-only lines).
// 3. Add a per-primitive elaborator dispatcher at
//    `crates/paideia-as-elaborator/src/stdlib_lowering/cryptoops/<name>.rs`
//    and expose it from `cryptoops/mod.rs` (append-only lines).
// 4. Add one `match` arm in `stdlib_lowering/mod.rs` — the only
//    shared-file touch, kept to a single line per primitive to
//    minimise merge surface.
// 5. Add this file's sibling `crypto/<name>.pdx` with the trait
//    declaration; document the register mapping and return codes.
// 6. Add a `pub use paideia_as_crypto::ffi::<thunk>;` line in
//    `crates/paideia-satellite-runtime/src/lib.rs` (append-only,
//    one line per thunk).
//
// Steps 1, 3, 5 land in new files that never collide with parallel
// work on other primitives; steps 2, 4, 6 are three trivial-merge
// touch points where per-primitive lines are kept in a single-line-
// per-symbol shape by construction.
